<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Silesian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1740">1740</a>–<a href="/years/1742">1742</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Silesian War was fought from 1740 to 1742 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> under Frederick II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>. Frederick invaded Silesia in December 1740, won at Mollwitz (1741) and Chotusitz (1742), and gained most of the province by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breslau">Treaty of Breslau</a>, confirmed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1742.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war formed one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> and was the first of three Silesian Wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a> held dynastic claims to several Silesian duchies, based in part on a 1537 inheritance treaty that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> rejected. Silesia was populous and prosperous, and the Upper Oder valley formed a natural military route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>.</p><p>Frederick II became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in May 1740 and inherited a large, well-trained army and a healthy treasury. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was in financial distress, and Emperor Charles VI died in October 1740 without a male heir. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> succeeded him under the Pragmatic Sanction of 1713, but Prussia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> contested it. Dynastic claims supplied a pretext, but Realpolitik and geostrategy played the leading part. On 11 December Frederick sent Maria Theresa an ultimatum demanding Silesia and, without waiting for a reply, advanced.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 16 December 1740, without a declaration of war, two Prussian corps totalling 27,000 men crossed into Silesia, defended by an Austrian garrison of 8,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> without a fight on 2 January 1741 and almost the whole province by the end of the month. An Austrian force under Wilhelm Reinhard von Neipperg broke the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> on 5 April, and the armies met at Mollwitz on 10 April. Frederick fled on Marshal Schwerin's advice, but the Prussians held the field and portrayed the battle as a victory.</p><p>Other powers were emboldened. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-breslau">Treaty of Breslau</a> of 5 June 1741, and Franco-Bavarian forces seized Linz on 14 September. By the secret Convention of Klein Schnellendorf of 9 October, Prussia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> ceased fighting in Silesia, but Frederick later repudiated it and advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. On 17 May 1742 Prince Charles Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> attacked at Chotusitz, and the Prussians won narrowly with heavy losses on both sides. Under British pressure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> made peace in the Treaty of Breslau of 11 June 1742, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-berlin-1742">Treaty of Berlin</a> of 28 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> ceded most of Silesia and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klodzko">Glatz</a>, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krnov">Jägerndorf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Troppau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a>. Prussia gained some 35,000 square kilometres and about a million subjects, but by making a separate peace it left its allies and gained a reputation for double-dealing. The war cost Austria its wealthiest province and some prestige, and began the Austria–Prussia rivalry that shaped German politics for over a century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s wish to recover Silesia led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-silesian-war">Second Silesian War</a> two years later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Silesian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the first major battle of the <b>First Silesian War</b>, the Prussian army of Frederick the Great defeated the Austrians under Neipperg, although Frederick had left the field when the battle seemed lost.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
